    h: 'Add a new note taker without code (platform admins)',
    tab: 'Overview',
    t: '7 Add a new note taker without code (platform admins) The point. A note taker that posts JSON to an address can be added from Settings by describing it: what it is called, how it signs its deliveries, which event to accept, and where the meeting fields live in its JSON. The same door, brain import and Meetings route handle it from then on. Only platform admins see the button; everyone sees the result. SituationDylan (platform admin) wants Otter meetings in ROAS. Otter is not in the Library. What happensIn Settings › Integrations he presses More integrations. A row of type cards appears: Note taker is clickable, the other kinds (Social media, CRM, Payments, ...) say Coming soon. He picks Note taker. The fastest way is at the top: he pastes one delivery from Otter (most tools have a "send test request" button), presses Detect fields, and ROAS fills the paths and reports what it found and what is missing; or he picks a template (Read AI style, Fireflies style, simple speakers-and-text) from the dropdown. Otherwise he fills the form by hand: name and logo; the signature rule (which header carries the HMAC, hex or base64, an optional prefix such as sha256=, or "no signature"); which event to accept (for tools that also post "meeting started" pings) and the delivery-id field; and the field map, written as dot paths with [] for lists, for example transcript.speaker_blocks[].words. The mapping test runs on the same sample (Test mapping at the bottom re-runs it after edits): the panel shows the meeting id, title, number of transcript turns, the first turn, attendees and action items it found. He presses Save note taker. What you seeThe dialog closes, the Library refreshes, and Otter sits under Productivity with its logo for every user. A user who presses Connect sees their personal ROAS address to paste into Otter first, then pastes Otter\'s secret and connects. Read AI and Fireflies now connect in the same order, which is what Read AI\'s own setup steps require (address first, key afterwards). Deliveries then flow like any other note taker. 📍 Screen: button web/features/settings/components/settings-content/IntegrationsView.tsx (rendered when isPlatformAdmin) · dialog web/features/settings/components/settings-content/more-integrations/MoreIntegrationsDialog.tsx · type cards more-integrations/integration-type-cards.ts · form more-integrations/NoteTakerDefinitionForm.tsx, field map NoteTakerFieldMapSection.tsx, test panel NoteTakerPreviewPanel.tsx, validation and API body use-note-taker-definition-form.ts · Library row from a definition web/lib/integrations/meeting-provider-definitions.ts definitionToIntegration · paste-and-detect more-integrations/NoteTakerStartSection.tsx → POST .../definitions/suggest api/meetings/custom/suggest-field-map.ts, templates GET .../definitions/templates api/meetings/custom/note-taker-templates.ts · address before Connect settings-content/MeetingWebhookAddressNote.tsx (any pasted-webhook tool: nt_, Read AI, Fireflies) 📍 Calls: GET/POST /integrations/meetings/definitions, POST .../definitions/preview api/meetings/intake/controllers/meeting-provider-definitions.controller.ts (writes need the platform admin role) · GET /integrations/meetings/:provider/webhook-address, POST .../connect, POST .../disconnect api/meetings/intake/controllers/meeting-connections.controller.ts · generic plug-in api/meetings/custom/custom-webhook-transcript-provider.ts, field map reader api/meetings/custom/json-path-lite.ts, resolver on the registry api/meetings/custom/custom-note-taker.resolver.ts 🗄️ Tables: meeting_provider_definitions migrations/20260915090000_meeting_provider_definitions.sql:6 · one integrations_available row per definition (id = the tool\'s nt_ slug) · vault_secrets label signing_key under the slug Limits today. A defined tool must push JSON to an address; tools that need a login page or polling still need a code plug-in. Defined tools have no past-meetings list in Train and Vibey cannot import from them by id yet. Both are logged follow-ups. Ids of defined tools start with nt_; api/meetings/providers/transcript-source.types.ts widens MeetingProviderId to built-ins plus that prefix, and meeting-provider.registry.ts resolve() asks the resolver for them with one database read per delivery, so an admin edit is visible on every API instance at once. Signature schemes: HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body (hex or base64, optional prefix, secret as text or base64) or none; Fathom-style Standard Webhooks is a logged follow-up.',
