    t: '2 How things get in and get read back Six stages, in this order. A door writes a ticket in the database. Redis is only the doorbell that tells a worker to look. A worker claims the ticket, Atlas reads the text, then a save tool checks the fingerprint, extracts, embeds, and inserts rows on a shelf. Click any door to jump to its story in section 3. Two doors (dashed) skip the ticket, the doorbell, and Atlas and write to the shelves directly. Doors ✍️ Typed or pasted text 📄 Uploaded file 🔗 Link 🎙️ Note taker meetingFathom, Fireflies, Read AI, or a tool an admin defined: one door for all 💬 Slack channel 🗂️ ROAS Portal (Page Grader)skips stages 1 to 4, writes straight to the shelves 🗨️ Chat with the helperskips stages 1 to 4, writes straight to the shelves ➜ Queue 1Ticket in the databaseOne row in brain_import_jobs, status queued. The truth about the job lives here: status, attempts, retry time, result. 2Doorbell in RedisEvery 3 seconds a sweep reads due tickets and drops a note per ticket into Redis. The note holds only the job id. 3ClaimA worker flips the row to processing. First writer wins; that single update is the lock. ➜ Reader 4Atlas reads itThe text goes to the Atlas agent in chunks, with a prompt that names exactly one save tool and the rule "keep only what still matters in 6 months". ➜ Save tool 5Fingerprintsha256 of the text. "Seen before?" If yes, stop here. Costs nothing. 5ExtractGemini pulls out the durable bits, each with a type and a score. 5Embedding768 numbers per bit so it can be found by meaning. Paid call. 5InsertOne row per bit, with its own hash and its embedding. ➜ Shelves 6📓 Memories 📚 SK textbook entries 💎 Snapshots (crystallized) 🧾 Evidence chunks (raw text) 🧭 Beliefs, perspectives, pages, timelines built later from many memories ➜ Readers 🖥️ Web app search 🧩 Chrome extension 🤖 The AI agent uses a different copy of the search code in another app; since 2026-09-15 that copy reads the personal brain and its textbook entries too (Live finding 4, Live finding 5) door work stage shelf (a table) reader skips the queue and Atlas One import, step by step The door writes the ticket. One row in brain_import_jobs with status queued, attempts 0, max 3. Nothing has touched Redis yet. 📍 brain/services/brain-import-jobs-runtime.base.ts:272 insertJob · 🗄️ brain_import_jobs in migrations/20260303120000_brain_import_jobs.sql:1 The doorbell rings. Right after the insert the api asks the sweep to look. Independently, a Redis timer named brain-import-sweep fires every 3 seconds. 📍 brain/services/brain-import-jobs-runtime.base.ts:306 processDueJobs · timer :167-174 installRuntimeSweep The sweep reads due tickets and drops one note per ticket into Redis. The note is { jobId } with one attempt and no retries. Redis is a pointer, never the record. 📍 brain/repositories/brain-import-jobs-runtime.repository.ts:48 findDueJobIds → brain/services/brain-import-jobs-runtime.base.ts:203-209 add(\'brain-import-job\') · queue name packages/api-shared/src/services/agent-runtime-queues.ts:13 A worker claims the ticket. One conditional update: set processing where status is still queued or retry. A second worker gets zero rows and drops the note. Two workers listen on this queue: the api itself and the mission worker. 📍 brain/repositories/brain-import-jobs-runtime.repository.ts:127-140 claimJob · workers brain/services/brain-import-runtime.processor.ts:14 and apps/mission-worker/src/modules/agent-runtime/processors/agent-runtime-brain-import.processor.ts:64 Atlas reads the text. Chunks of 350,000 characters. The prompt names exactly one save tool for the target brain. The call goes to agent-api, then to the OpenClaw gateway, with a 15-minute limit. 📍 brain/services/brain-import-jobs-execution.base.ts:59 chunking · :196-249 buildAtlasSystemPrompt one tool per brain · apps/api/src/modules/missions/services/gateways/mission-agent-gateway.service.ts:225 callOpenClawForBrainJob Atlas calls the save tool. The tool lives in agent-api: save_user_memory for a personal brain, ingest_agent_brain_text for an agent brain, save_customer_memory for the customer brain. 📍 apps/agent-api/src/modules/artifacts/services/artifact-action.registry.ts:597-641 Fingerprint first. The tool hashes the whole text and asks "seen before in this brain?". A duplicate stops here before any paid call. 📍 apps/agent-api/src/modules/brain/services/sk-ingestion.service.ts:66-73 · personal brain apps/agent-api/src/modules/artifacts/services/artifact-legacy-team-brain-memory.service.ts:67-77 · 🗄️ ns_content_hashes in migrations/047_brain_consolidation.sql:420 Extract, embed, insert. Gemini pulls the durable bits; each bit gets its own hash, 768 numbers from gemini-embedding-2, and one row on the shelf. 📍 apps/agent-api/src/modules/brain/services/sk-ingestion.service.ts:132,134,143,148 · model brain/services/embedding.service.ts:7,10 Atlas reports, the ticket closes. Atlas must end with JOB_STATUS:completed, failed, or skipped. The row becomes succeeded, or retry with a wait, or failed after 3 attempts. A notification row is written. 📍 brain/services/brain-import-jobs-execution.base.ts:278 · brain/services/brain-import-jobs-runtime.base.ts:355 succeeded · :404 failed or retry · :377 notification 🗄️ The database is the ticket Everything that matters about a job is in the row: status, how many attempts, when to try again, the error, the result. Retry waits are computed here, not in Redis. If two processes share this database, they share the tickets, which is exactly Live finding 2. 📍 brain/services/brain-import-jobs-runtime.base.ts:404-408 markJobFailed · backoff table brain/services/brain-import-jobs.base.ts:174-183 · 🗄️ brain_import_jobs status check in migrations/20260303120000_brain_import_jobs.sql:8 🔔 Redis is the doorbell Redis holds a repeating 3-second timer and one tiny note per due ticket. The note carries only the job id, one attempt, and is deleted when done. If Redis forgets everything, no job is lost: the next sweep reads the tickets again. 📍 brain/services/brain-import-jobs-runtime.base.ts:167-184 timers · :203-209 the note · Redis address order packages/api-shared/src/services/agent-runtime-queues.ts:28 SituationRedis restarts while three tickets are queued and one is processing. What happensThe notes in Redis vanish. The three rows still say queued. When the api reconnects, the 3-second timer is registered again on start and the next sweep drops three fresh notes. The processing row keeps running; if its worker died, the 20-minute stale check moves it back to retry. What you seeJobs resume within seconds. No duplicate rows, because the claim is one conditional update. 📍 brain/services/brain-import-jobs-runtime.base.ts:20 onModuleInit · stale recovery :218 recoverStaleJobs, window brain/services/brain-import-jobs.base.ts:25-27 Want the full detail? The Flow tab has every step with the store it touches, which door takes which road, and what each shelf really is.',
